
                          INTERMEDIATE CATALOG FOR ENCODER

DESCRIPTION

The encoder takes the external standard intermediate and re-encodes it to an
internal four operand intermediate entry format. In addition, there are several
custom intermediate entries that are created within the encoder. These entries
contain simplified sequences such as load address and fetch concatenated,
conditional jumps, and other special operators. These operators will only be
found in the encoder, and are not standard intermediates. There are also several
external standard intermediates that are not used internally in the encoder.
These are noted in the catalog.

APPLICABILITY

This information is needed by anyone modifying or creating a backend for the
encoder. This file essentially describes the intermediate graph operator entries
for the intermediate graph that is passed to the backend on the block stack
by a genblk call.

PROCESSING

An encoder backend can be implemented by either stack based or register based
archtecture, or even some other method. The type system gives the list of
types and variables for each block that is accessable in the scope of the
intermediate being processed.

If the intermediate graph passed is to be understood in stack based form, then
the graph should be traversed in standard left or right ordering, then the
loads, stores and operators work in terms of the stack.

If the intermediate graph is to be understood in register based form, the
registers are assigned using the standard intermediate fields provided for that.
Then the graph can be traversed in any order. In this mode, the loads and
stores are understood in terms of to or from a register. Items that cannot
fit in registers are typically allocated as temps, then the register becomes
the address of that. In this way, any size operand can be handled.

There are several fields in the intermediate that are used by the standard
register allocator, which is contained in the machine dependent section of
encode. Sourcewise, the machine indepenent section of encode does not
know about or use these fields, and they may, in fact, be unused if the
machine dependent section is generating a stack based model, for example.

GRAPH FORM

The intermediate is in a form termed "acyclic graph", which is a tree with a
single root with N-single branches. It is acyclic because all program 
structures, including loops such as for or repeat, are represented by single
branches. This is possible because the final code generation treats the single
branch as a list that is to be repeated. Gotos do form cycles, but with side
links that are not part of the intermediate graph form.

The graph is separated into terminal and expression operators, and this is
very important to the understanding of the intermediate graph. The terminal
operators form the backbone of the program structure, and represent operators
that deliver no result value, such as assignment, read and write, etc. The
expression operators form expression trees that are rooted into the terminal
structure.

CATALOG

************************* Expression Operators *********************************

tilodadr     load address operator 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.
Base:  Type of object to be loaded.

The address of the object described by the type is loaded.

tilodfadr    load address function result operator 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.
Base:  Type of object to be loaded.

The address of the object described by the type is loaded. The type will be the
type of the function result for the current function.

tiarrref     array reference operator 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.
Base:  Array type
Left:  Array base address
Right: Index

The index for the array is scaled by the size of the array element size, then
used to offset into the array. The result is the address of the object in the
array.

tiarfgar     general array reference operator 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.
Base:  Array type
Left:  Array base address
Right: Index

The index for the array is scaled by the size of the array element size, then
used to offset into the array. The result is the address of the object in the
array. The address of the array is a tagged pointer, allowing the reference
to be checked against the number of elements in the array.

tirecoff     record offset operator 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.
Base:  Record field entry
Left:  Record base address

The offset for the record field is added to the base address. The result is an
address of the field.

tildiint     load indirect integer operator 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.
Base:  Type of integer to load (subrange)
Left:  Address of integer to load

The size of integer indicated by the type is loaded from the address. If the
register or stack has a standard size requirement, sign or zero extention is
done as appropriate to the type of integer subrange.

tildirel     load indirect real operator 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.
Left:  Address of real to load

The standard length real is loaded from its address.

tildisrl     load indirect short real operator 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.
Left:  Address of short real to load

The short length real is loaded from its address. If the register or stack has
a standard length requirement, short real is converted to standard real at this
step.

tildiset     load indirect set operator 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.
Left:   Address of set to load

The set at the address is loaded. The set is loaded to 256 element standard
form.

tildichr     load indirect character operator 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.
Left:  Address of character to load

The character at the address is loaded, and zero extended as required.

tildibol     load indirect boolean operator 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.
Left:  Address of boolean to load

The boolean at the address is loaded, and zero extended as required.

tildisrc     load indirect structure operator 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.
Base:  Type of structure to load
Left:  Address of structure to load

The structure at the address is loaded, with the size of the structure type.

tildiptr     load indirect pointer 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.

tilditgp     load indirect tagged pointer 
================================================================================
Rbase: Type of the resulting object.

